language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ divisible_by_1_through_9: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Find a 10 digit number that uses each of the digits 0 to 9 exactly once and where the number formed by the first n digits of the number is divisible by n. Read the digits from left to right: the first digit is divisible by 1, the first two digits form a number divisible by 2, and so on, up to the whole 10 digit number, which is divisible by 10.
$
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/divisible_by_1_through_9.html
$ Save this model as model.eprime and run it with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the solution is
$ written to `model.eprime.solution`:
$   savilerow model.eprime -run-solver

$ digits[i]: the i-th digit of the number, read from left to right, indices 1-based (the output)
find digits : matrix indexed by [int(1..10)] of int(0..9)

branching on [digits]
such that
$ the 10 digits are all different, so each of the digits 0 to 9 is used exactly once
allDiff(digits),
$ the first digit is not zero, so the number has 10 digits
digits[1] != 0,
$ for each n from 1 to 10, the number formed by the first n digits is divisible by n
forAll n : int(1..10) . (sum i : int(1..n) . digits[i] * 10**(n - i)) % n = 0
